"""Flavor texts: root, Modell Deutschland and the energy programme."""
D = {
"social_market": "Twenty-five years after Ludwig Erhard abolished rationing overnight, the social market economy is the Federal Republic's founding myth: full employment, a hard Deutschmark and an export surplus that the world envies. But the miracle years are over. Growth is slowing, the guest workers are staying, and the unions want their share of the wealth they helped to create.",
"concerted_action": "Government, employers, unions and the Bundesbank at one table: Karl Schiller's Concerted Action is an attempt to steer wages, prices and growth together rather than against each other. Its guidelines are only recommendations, and every side reads them differently. But as long as everyone keeps coming to the table, the Federal Republic avoids the strikes that paralyse its neighbours.",
"public_sector_strike": "February 1974, in the middle of the oil shock: the public-service union ÖTV demands double-digit pay rises and brings the bin lorries, the trams and the airports to a standstill. Willy Brandt has publicly warned against such a settlement. If he gives way, his authority is broken; if he holds firm, the strike may break his government first.",
"stimulus_1975": "The first real recession since the war: a million unemployed, short-time work in every industrial town, factories idle. The government reaches for Keynes - investment grants, construction programmes, regional aid - and borrows to pay for it. The Bundesbank watches with folded arms, ready to raise interest rates the moment it sees inflation.",
"austerity_1975": "Hold the line. The Budget Structure Act cuts subsidies, freezes posts in the civil service and trims the social budget, so that the state lives within its means and the Bundesbank can do its work. The economists in Frankfurt approve. The unemployed, whose number is still rising, are less impressed.",
"locomotive": "At the Bonn summit of 1978 the Americans want Germany and Japan to become the locomotives of the world economy: more demand at home to pull the others out of recession. Helmut Schmidt reluctantly agrees to a stimulus programme worth one per cent of GDP. In return he wants Carter to raise American oil prices. Both sides will regret the bargain.",
"bundesbank_independence": "The Bundesbank is independent by law, by conviction and by popular demand: the Germans remember two inflations that destroyed their savings within a generation. Chancellors who fight it lose, sooner or later. Respecting Frankfurt costs growth and jobs in a downturn - but buys a currency that the whole world wants to hold.",
"end_bretton_woods": "The dollar can no longer hold the system of fixed exchange rates. Speculators buy Deutschmarks by the billion, the Bundesbank prints marks to buy dollars, and imported inflation floods the country. In March 1973 the D-Mark is set free to float - and it rises, and rises. German exports become more expensive; German holidays, cheaper.",
"ems": "Helmut Schmidt and Valéry Giscard d'Estaing, two former finance ministers, design a new monetary order for Europe: the currencies of the Community bound together in a grid of fixed but adjustable rates. Officially the grid is anchored to a basket called the ECU. In practice everyone knows it is anchored to the D-Mark - and to the Bundesbank's interest rates.",
"export_world_champion": "Machine tools from Swabia, chemicals from the Rhine, cars from Wolfsburg and Stuttgart: German industry outsells everyone, dollar or no dollar. 'Made in Germany', once a British warning label, has become a promise of quality. The trade surplus is the pride of the Republic - and an irritation to every trading partner that has to finance it.",
"working_time_385": "Spring 1984: IG Metall strikes for seven weeks for the 35-hour week, the employers lock out hundreds of thousands, and the car industry grinds to a halt. Georg Leber mediates the compromise: 38.5 hours, with flexible working time at plant level. Both sides claim victory. The road to the 35-hour week is open.",
"neue_heimat_cleanup": "Neue Heimat, the trade unions' housing company, built four hundred thousand homes for working families. Now Der Spiegel reveals that its managers lined their own pockets while the tenants paid. The DGB's empire is rotten, and the SPD stands too close to it. A clean liquidation now may be the only way to stop the scandal from eating the party.",
"privatisation_wave": "Why should the state own oil companies, aluminium smelters, a car manufacturer and an airline? The government sells its stakes in VEBA, VIAG, Volkswagen and Lufthansa, brings in some ten billion marks for the treasury and turns hundreds of thousands of citizens into shareholders. The unions warn about job cuts; the Bundestag's free-market wing wants more.",
"postal_reform": "The Bundespost is a giant: letters, savings accounts and the telephone network in a single public authority with half a million employees. The reform splits it into three companies - post, bank and telecom - and prepares them for the market. In 1996 the Deutsche Telekom share is floated as 'the people's share'. Millions buy. Not all will be glad they did.",
"single_market": "The Single European Act of 1986 promises a Europe without internal borders by the end of 1992: goods, capital, services and people moving freely across the Community. For Germany's exporters it is the largest home market in the world. For Germany's beer brewers, insurers and craftsmen, it is the end of a great many comfortable national rules.",
"tax_reform_1990": "Gerhard Stoltenberg's tax reform lowers income tax in three stages, cutting the entry rate and the top rate alike - the largest tax relief in the Republic's history so far. It is meant to reward effort and to bring the economy out of its stagnation. The opposition calls it a gift for the rich; the economists, overdue.",
"monetary_union_offer": "'If the D-Mark comes, we stay. If it doesn't, we go to it.' Every week tens of thousands of East Germans leave for the West. Bonn's answer is the boldest economic decision since 1948: one currency for both German states, decided in months, at an exchange rate the East's factories cannot survive. The Bundesbank's president warns. The chancellor overrules him.",
"treuhand": "Eight and a half thousand state enterprises, four million employees, an economy built for the Comecon market that no longer exists. The Treuhandanstalt is to privatise, restructure or close them all. Its first president is murdered by the RAF; its second sells off the East's industry at a pace that leaves whole regions without work. The bill arrives with the unemployment.",
"solidarity_pact": "Unity costs more than anyone admitted in 1990. The Solidarity Pact of 1993 is the answer: a solidarity surcharge on income tax, the German Unity Fund, and a new financial settlement between the Länder that brings the East into the federal equalisation. The West pays, the East receives, and both sides feel that the other is ungrateful.",
"aufbau_ost": "Motorways and telephone lines, universities and hospitals, rebuilt city centres in Görlitz, Weimar and Stralsund: the reconstruction of the East becomes the largest transfer of wealth in German history - more than a trillion marks within a decade. Some of it builds the future. Some of it builds empty industrial parks in fields of rapeseed.",
"euro_founder": "The Germans love their D-Mark more than their flag. Giving it up for a common European currency is the price of unity that Paris has asked for - and the Federal Republic sets its own terms: a currency built in the D-Mark's image, an independent central bank in Frankfurt, and a stability pact that will bind every member to budgetary discipline. The Bundestag votes for it. The country is less sure.",
"dmark_bloc": "If France says no to Maastricht, there is another way: a smaller hard-currency zone around the D-Mark, with the Benelux countries, Austria and Denmark. A core Europe that works, rather than a big Europe that fails. In Paris and Rome it would look like what it is - a German sphere of influence with a central bank in Frankfurt.",
"agenda_reform": "High wages, high taxes, high social contributions and four million unemployed: in the 1990s the debate about Standort Deutschland - Germany as a place to do business - dominates every talk show. Companies threaten to move to Hungary or Alabama. The government must decide how much of the old Modell Deutschland it is willing to sacrifice to keep them.",
"petersberg_reform": "On the Petersberg above Bonn, the coalition designs a great tax reform for 1998: lower rates, fewer exemptions, a simpler system. It passes the Bundestag - and dies in the Bundesrat, where Oskar Lafontaine's SPD majority blocks it to deny the chancellor a success before the election. Germany has a new word for its condition: Reformstau, the reform jam.",
"steel_crisis": "The steelworks of the Saar and the Ruhr are losing to Japan, Korea and Brazil, which produce cheaper and newer. Mergers, subsidies and quotas from Brussels are meant to slow the decline; in 1978 IG Metall calls the first long strike in the steel industry for the 35-hour week. Whole towns live from the blast furnaces. What happens to them when the fires go out?",
"ruhr_structural_change": "Universities instead of coal pits, shopping centres instead of blast furnaces, a landscape park where the coking plant used to be. North Rhine-Westphalia sets out to reinvent its industrial heartland: slowly, expensively, with the unions at the table and the miners guaranteed that nobody will fall into unemployment. It takes a generation. Perhaps two.",
"shipyard_crisis": "AG Weser in Bremen, one of the great shipyards of the world, closes in 1983 after a hundred and forty years. Asian yards build cheaper, and the merchant fleets no longer need German ships. Bremen, Kiel and Hamburg ask Bonn for money to save what is left. The question is whether the money buys a future or only time.",
"guest_worker_policy": "The recruitment stop of November 1973 ends the hiring of guest workers - but not the immigration. Those who are here stay, because leaving would mean never coming back, and their families follow. The Federal Republic is becoming a country of immigration, with Turkish grocers in Kreuzberg and Italian ice-cream parlours in every town. It refuses to admit it.",
"return_premium": "10,500 marks, plus 1,500 for every child, for every guest worker who goes home for good. The Return Assistance Act of 1983 is the government's attempt to reverse the immigration it never admitted to. Some 250,000 people take the money and leave. Most of the others stay - now with the knowledge that the state would prefer them gone.",
"research_policy": "Data processing programmes, nuclear research centres in Jülich and Karlsruhe, the Max Planck Society for basic research and the Fraunhofer institutes for application: the federal research ministry makes the state into a venture capitalist for technologies that no company would finance alone. Some bets pay off; others become museums.",
"airbus": "European champions where national ones failed: in Toulouse and Hamburg, the Airbus consortium builds airliners to challenge Boeing; in Kourou, in French Guiana, Ariane rockets launch European satellites. Germany pays a large share and gets a large share of the work. The rest of the world calls it subsidy. Europe calls it sovereignty.",
"microelectronics": "Japan and America lead in microchips, and whoever leads in chips will lead in everything else. A federal programme and Siemens' megabit project, together with Philips, try to close the gap. Germany is excellent at building the machines that make products. Whether it can master the products that make the machines think is another question.",
"high_speed_rail": "Hanover to Würzburg, Mannheim to Stuttgart: the first entirely new railway lines built in Germany since the days of the Empire, with tunnels through every hill and bridges over every valley, ready for trains at 250 kilometres an hour. In 1991 the InterCity Express goes into service. The railway is back in the age of the motorway.",
"vocational_training": "Two days at the vocational school, three days at the workbench: the dual system of apprenticeship trains two-thirds of every generation as skilled workers and is the quiet strength behind 'Made in Germany'. Youth unemployment is lower than anywhere else in Europe. Other countries send delegations to study it; few manage to copy it.",
"housing_programme": "Märkisches Viertel, Neuperlach, Chorweiler: satellite towns at the edge of every big city, with concrete towers, district heating and underground car parks. In 1972 they are modern and a promise of decent housing for all. By 1985 many have become problem estates. The architects have moved on; the tenants have not.",
"job_creation": "Two million unemployed, and many of them for years. The Federal Labour Office pays local authorities and charities to hire them for a year: to plant trees, renovate schools, sort archives, care for the elderly. It is not a real job, say the critics. For those who have it, it is a reason to get up in the morning.",
"early_retirement": "1984: workers may retire at fifty-eight if their employer hires a young unemployed person in their place. The unions call it solidarity between the generations; the companies use it to shed older workers at the expense of the pension funds. It lowers the unemployment figures. It also raises a bill that later governments will have to pay.",
"working_longer": "Fewer children, longer lives, and a pension system that pays today's retirees out of today's contributions. The pension reform of 1992 raises the retirement age step by step and ties pensions to net rather than gross wages. It is the first great reform that the opposition helps to write - and it is passed on the very day the Berlin Wall falls.",
"energy_programme": "October 1973: the Arab oil embargo turns the lights off on the motorways and puts car-free Sundays on the calendar. The government's energy programme promises a way out: away from oil, coal for security, nuclear power for growth, natural gas from Holland, Norway - and the Soviet Union. Every option has a price, and every price has a constituency against it.",
"kohlepfennig": "A few pfennigs on every electricity bill, paid by every household in the country, so that the power stations burn German hard coal instead of cheaper imports. The 'coal penny' keeps the pits of the Ruhr and the Saar open and two hundred thousand miners in work. It is security of supply, says the government. It is a subsidy, says Brussels.",
"century_contract": "The power utilities commit themselves to burning German hard coal for fifteen years, in rising quantities, at prices far above the world market - the 'contract of the century'. The miners have their future, the Ruhr its stability, the government its security of supply. The electricity customers pay the premium without being asked.",
"waldsterben": "'The forest is dying', Der Spiegel announces on its cover: needles falling, crowns thinning, acid rain from the power stations' chimneys. No issue has moved the Germans like this one; the forest is a part of their soul. Environmental policy leaves the fringes and enters the chancellery. The power stations get scrubbers, the cars catalytic converters.",
"nuclear_buildout": "Biblis, Brokdorf, Grafenrheinfeld, Isar: the energy programme foresees fifty reactors by 1985, and with them cheap electricity, independence from the oil sheikhs and the world's best nuclear industry. Every new site brings a construction fence - and on the other side of the fence, a few thousand people who refuse to accept it.",
"fast_breeder": "Kalkar's fast breeder, the thorium reactor at Hamm, a reprocessing plant at Wackersdorf: the dream of a plutonium economy that produces more fuel than it consumes. They are the great prestige projects of German nuclear research - billions of marks invested, decades of planning. They may never deliver a single kilowatt-hour to the grid.",
"nuclear_moratorium": "Wyhl, Brokdorf, Grohnde, Gorleben: wherever a reactor is to be built, the citizens' initiatives occupy the site. The government can keep fighting them with water cannon - or listen. Finish what is under construction, build nothing new, and let the next generation decide whether the nuclear age has a future in Germany.",
"chernobyl_response": "April 1986: a reactor explodes in the Ukraine, and a few days later a radioactive cloud drifts over Bavaria. Contaminated milk, children kept indoors, playgrounds closed, a run on iodine tablets, and authorities that give contradictory advice. The anti-nuclear movement was right, say millions of Germans who never marched with it. The government has to answer.",
"gas_for_pipes": "February 1970: Mannesmann supplies large-diameter steel pipes, German banks provide the credit, and the Soviet Union pays with natural gas delivered through those same pipes for twenty years. The first great deal of Ostpolitik binds the Ruhr to Siberia. Washington is suspicious. The Ruhr's steelworkers are delighted.",
"urengoy": "A credit of 3.4 billion marks for a pipeline from the gas fields of Urengoy to Western Europe. The Reagan administration wants it stopped: it would make Europe dependent on Moscow and fill the Soviet treasury with hard currency. Bonn builds it anyway. It is the first time the Federal Republic openly defies Washington on a matter of economic security.",
"energy_savings_act": "The cheapest energy is the energy that is never used. Insulation standards for every new building, efficiency rules for heating boilers, a ministry that counts kilowatt-hours. It is not glamorous - no reactors, no pipelines - but within ten years the Federal Republic needs a fifth less oil to produce the same output.",
"environment_ministry": "Four weeks after Chernobyl, the Federal Republic finally has a minister for the environment, nature conservation and reactor safety. Walter Wallmann's ministry is to bring together what was scattered across the interior, agriculture and health ministries. For the first time, the environment has a seat at the cabinet table - and a budget to defend.",
"green_dot": "Packaging must be taken back by whoever sells it: the Packaging Ordinance and the Duales System with its Green Dot make recycling a national habit. Every German kitchen now has four bins, and every German citizen an opinion on the correct way to sort a yoghurt pot. The rest of Europe watches with amusement - and then copies it.",
"renewable_research": "Growian, the giant wind turbine on the North Sea coast, is a failure: too big, too early, constantly breaking down. The research ministry's solar and wind programmes produce more reports than electricity. But the engineers learn, and the lessons of Growian's failure will be built into a generation of smaller, better machines.",
"feed_in_law": "A backbench law, passed almost without debate in December 1990: the utilities must buy electricity from wind and solar plants at a fixed price. Two members of the Bundestag, one CSU and one Green, wrote it together. Nobody in the energy companies takes it seriously. It will change the German landscape more than any reactor.",
"eco_tax": "Make energy more expensive and labour cheaper: the ecological tax reform raises taxes on petrol, heating oil and electricity step by step and uses the revenue to lower pension contributions. It is the red-green government's first big bet. The motorists' club rages, the tabloids count every pfennig, and the climate scientists applaud.",
"atomkonsens": "A negotiated exit rather than a fight: every reactor is allotted a remaining quantity of electricity it may still produce, and none will get a successor. The utilities sign because the alternative is worse; the Greens sign because it is better than nothing. The last German reactor will be switched off in about twenty years. The anti-nuclear movement calls it far too slow.",
}
